WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:05.310
One day I was walking home from grocery shopping
with heaps of heavy bags and so I decided

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.250
to give some thought to which way home would
be the shortest.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.230
Well, the shortest route would definitely
have been a straight diagonal path from the

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.640
shops to my house, but unfortunately no one
had made that path for me.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.670
Instead there were only paths that went north-south
and east-west.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.681
'Surely some of these paths are better than
others," I reasoned, "because the diagonal

00:00:25.681 --> 00:00:30.000
line is the best, so routes which are almost
diagonal should be better than going all the

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.700
way around, right?"

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.750
At this point my brain started to hurt so
I forgot all this and just trudged back home

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.400
the regular way

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.310
When I got home though, I got interested in
the question again.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.170
But I decided to make the problem a bit simpler.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.730
I want to find out the shortest paths between
this corner and this corner of a square but

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.900
no diagonals are allowed, and the paths can
only go left or up.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.920
This is the longest path from A to B, two
units long.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.649
The would-be shortest path is the diagonal,
which by trusty Pythagoras is root 2 units

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:01.829
long.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:03.920
Lets draw some other random path.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.810
What's the total distance in the left direction?

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.450
Well, I guess its one unit.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.319
And the distance in the up direction is 1
also.

00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:15.939
Making the total distance 2, just like the
long way around The same thing is true for

00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:16.939
all the paths.

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:21.420
I was completely wrong, there is no shortest
path because all those paths are exactly the

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.119
same length

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:25.520
To you this is probably like, yeah obviously.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.130
For me this is still so surprising.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.070
Let me explain.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.920
The diagonal is the shortest route, but we
can draw one of our allowed paths that approximations

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:35.920
it.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.670
You might not think this is a good approximation,
but I can make it better and better and better,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.319
until your eyes couldn't even tell the difference.

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:49.159
In fact, couldn't I make the approximation
so good, someone walking on it couldn't even

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:51.700
tell they're not quite walking on a straight
line?

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.490
But then, they have to be able to tell because
no matter how great the approximation is,

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.520
these paths all have length 2 which is way
longer than root 2 so this way they'll end

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.669
up walking so much extra distance than if
they were actually on the diagonal.

00:02:06.669 --> 00:02:09.110
That's the bit that really messes with my
mind.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:14.629
Another weird bit is that in some ways the
sequence is a good approximation for the line.

00:02:14.629 --> 00:02:19.459
If you know calculus or have seen last episode,
you know that if what I cared about was the

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:24.290
area underneath the diagonal instead of the
diagonal's length, this stair case shape has

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.090
area that gets closer and closer to the area
we want.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.579
So we have this sequence of shapes that visually
approximate the line very well, and we know

00:02:32.579 --> 00:02:35.450
it approximates its area very well.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.680
And yet it's terrible at approximating the
line's length.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.750
This is a really worrying example.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.390
How do we know in advance when an approximation
is not good enough?

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.440
I really don't know.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.520
So even though we all use approximation a
lot with out a second thought, even approximations

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.049
that look like they're really good can go
completely wrong.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:59.900
Well, there are lots of places we go from
here because this whole thing brings up heaps

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.540
of questions.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.680
One bit I'm really interested in is the fact
that the shape we were talking about is actually

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.170
a fractal.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:12.239
Fractals in general are kind of the arch nememis
of Calculus, so we might take up the story

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:13.519
there next time.

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:13.989
See you then!

